hi I'm Eric Voss and the movie Logan  made news recently for being the first  film of 2017 to send out Oscar screeners  now screeners are the DVDs that movie  studios sent out to promote a film as  being worthy of being nominated for an  Academy Award  normally this process happens later in  the fall every year but 20th Century Fox  wanted to get a head start and get Logan  and voters minds so this brings up one  of the biggest questions we've had in  2017 could Logan win an Oscar will it be  the first superhero movie to win Best  Picture in the past the superhero film  genre has been notoriously shut out from  the Oscar race many have gotten  nominated for technical awards like  visual effects but only a small number  of DC Marvel adaptations have actually  won the original Richard Donner Superman  won for visual effects Tim Burton's 1989  Batman won for art direction spider-man  2 won for visual effects The Dark Knight  won for Best Supporting Actor and for  sound editing and believe it or not  Suicide Squad won remember for makeup  and hairstyling which I guess makes  sense now of course many critics felt  the Dark Knight deserved to be nominated  for Best Picture and arguably it was its  snub that was the reason the Academy  switched to nominating up to 10 films  the following year now a big reason for  this trend is winning Oscars just isn't  what superhero movies typically aim to  do Studios like Marvel and Warner  Brothers and Fox really just wanted to  adapt comic book figures into  commercially successful films and  franchises studios see them as a way to  make money and often that comes at odds  with winning over 5,000 or so retirees  in LA who see a Marvel movies huge  financial profits as enough of a reward  and instead prefer to nominate a film  they see as more artistically  sophisticated and if what I just said  sounds super technical and political  that's because that's what the Oscars  are Academy voters don't reward the  truly best picture of the year they  reward whatever film they feel in that  year reflects on their moral conscience  but here's why some people think Logan  could break through all that it has a  few key strengths that you tend to see  in past oscar-nominated films for one  it's r-rated all of the recent Best  Picture winners have been r-rated and  allows films to explore more adult  themes and film goers tend to take them  more seriously also it stars Hugh  Jackman and Patrick Stewart in meaty  challenging roles and both actors are  considered Hollywood darlings whom the  Academy would presume  love to reward jackman even hosted the  Oscars in 2009 then Logan was also  directed by James Mangold's he's a heavy  hitter who's well regarded by Academy  voters from his work on the  award-winning walk the line and yeah  it's a really good film  it's a powerful redemption tale that  doesn't require a fanboys obsession with  comic-book mythology to appreciate it  however despite all those strengths I  don't think those will be enough to  guarantee Logan an Oscar nomination  instead I think there's one other key  element about this movie that gives it a  major edge Logan transforms its genre so  when Logan came out earlier this year a  lot of reviews were pointing out the  movies parallels with the classic  western Shane Shane even gets referenced  in the movie itself but there's another  famous Western that I think is an even  closer spiritual ancestor Unforgiven the  film starring and directed by Clint  Eastwood I rewatched it recently and  it's incredible and one of the things  Unforgiven did so well was it  deconstructed the tropes and stereotypes  of the western genre and used them to  tell a more grounded human story that  defied those traditions Eastwood plays  William Munny a washed-up former  gunslinger trying to escape his violent  past and these sensational tales that  people tell about him but he gets sucked  into a bloody conflict where he reverts  back into the animal people expect him  to be in Unforgiven killing isn't a fun  spectacle it's agonizing for the victim  and traumatic for the killer no one in  the film is a clear-cut good guy it's a  hell of a thing killing a man take away  all he's got and all he's ever gonna  this was a huge departure from every  previous Western movies actually that  made Clint Eastwood a star those all had  clear white hats and black hats and wild  shootouts with countless nobody's dying  without a second buy and it was largely  for this reason that many believe the  Academy went so apeshit for Unforgiven  they awarded at four Oscars Best Picture  Best Director Best Supporting Actor and  best editing to the voters Unforgiven  was transformative  Eastwood deconstructed the popularity of  his homes onra to produce something  deeper and more meaningful and one might  argue artistically redeem any  unsophisticated critics saw in the  western genre Unforgiven was the hero  a broader Hollywood narrative now the  same could be argued for Logan like  William Munny Logan is a washed-up  superhero trying to escape his violent  past who scoffs at the fish stories  people tell about him we got ourselves  an x-men fan maybe a quarter of it  happened and look at this in the real  world people die Hugh Jackman is two  superhero movies what Clint Eastwood was  to Western as Wolverine in the first  x-men in 2000 he was the guy who started  it all the guy who made the genre  popular and liked Eastwood and  Unforgiven Jackman uses Logan to ask  what it means to be a superhero to have  real blood on your hands and to carry  the emotional weight that any real human  would feel if they actually lived  through the bloodshed that these Marvel  and DC characters experienced all the  time  Logan is a richly human response to and  reflection on the superhero genre as a  whole the movies had have dominated the  film industry for the past decade it's a  movie about movies about the stories we  tell and our responsibility to tell them  right and unforgiven showed that that is  exactly the kind of film that film  critics and Oscar voters tend to embrace  when award season comes around  now of course Logan isn't the only good  film to come out in 2017 it's gonna be  tough to stand out in a crowded field of  films like Chris Nolan's Dunkirk which  used an inventive structure to document  a critical moment in history and Jordan  peels get out which was an amazing  horror film about racial tensions  not to mention Steven Spielberg's  upcoming the post starring Tom Hanks and  Meryl Streep as Nixon era Washington  Post editors I'm just saying don't be  surprised if politics plays a big role  in the Oscars next year I just hope  Logan can stay part of the conversation  maybe at least to recognize Jackman or  Stewart for their incredible  performances like I don't need superhero  movies to win Oscars  I'm just happy they exist at all and  they're enjoyable for the most part but  it would be nice for a film that rises  above the genre to be recognized for its  artistic achievement but I'm gonna throw  it to you  what other superhero movies do you think  deserved a Best Picture nomination  obviously the Dark Knight has been part  of that conversation forever but there's  also a lot of buzz around Wonder Woman  from this year and guys let's be honest  Howard the Duck Maj snug this your  favorites in the comments below or by  tweeting me at da boss or follow new  rockstars on twitter at new rockstars  for updates on our videos and share this  video  and subscribe to our channel for more  deep dives and the movies you love Andy  going to help us make more videos like  this you can contribute to us on patreon  big thanks to all of our current donors  especially Kristina Armstrong alright  thanks for watching bite
 

